		<td class="row1" width="100%" height="28" valign="top"><table width="100%" border="0" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0">
			<tr>
				<td width="100%"><a href="viewtopic.php?p=144831&amp;sid=c67756e86843124b513922a42717c7aa#144831"><img src="templates/subSilver/images/icon_minipost.gif" width="12" height="9" alt="Post" title="Post" border="0" /></a><span class="postdetails">Posted: Mon Feb 10, 2003 1:52 am<span class="gen">&nbsp;</span>&nbsp; &nbsp;Post subject: /mourn Pentium 4 chip</span></td>
				<td valign="top" align="right" nowrap="nowrap"><a href="posting.php?mode=quote&amp;p=144831&amp;sid=c67756e86843124b513922a42717c7aa"><img src="templates/subSilver/images/lang_english/icon_quote.gif" alt="Reply with quote" title="Reply with quote" border="0" /></a>   </td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td colspan="2"><hr /></td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody">I witnessed the death of an Intel chip today.  It wasn't so bad, since I don't like Intel products much, but the problem with this chip was that it belonged to a customer.  The system had a defective motherboard, and was brough to us in the middle of January.  We ordered the part, and everything kinda got lost in the paperwork.  The customer called us today and asked if it was fixed yet - it was then that we realized we still had the computer with us.  We told them the part was arriving tomorrow, and we'd get it done right away...
<br />

<br />
...so Raj runs over and grabs the system, lays everthing out on the counter, and proudly says the system will be booting in 7 minutes.  He has Rob and I time him.  Off he goes, hooking everything up, and things are going well until he gets to the chip itself.  It's glued to the heatsink, and attempts to get it off fail.  Not wanting to pry it off with a screwdriver, starts just trying to attach it to the socket while it's on the heatsink.  Eventually it goes into place - but what none of us saw was the socket clamp still closed.  Yeah, he forgot to lift it up, seat the chip, then drop it back down.  Only a few of the pins would have made contact with the socket....
<br />

<br />
Thirteen minutes into it, Raj pokes the power button and... nada.  Zip.  No power.  We fiddle with the power connections, and try again.  Still nothing.  So now he thinks that maybe he didn't get the chip on right, so he starts taking it off... that's when we smelt the burning.  There was a great deal of screaming, swearing and laughing as we lifted the chip off and saw a bunch of the pins were black and melted into little piles, and some of them actually broke off in the socket.  
<br />

<br />
So now we're ordering a new chip, and another motherboard.  Luckily it's still under warranty, so there is no cost to the customer or our store.
<br />

<br />
Oh yeah, and Raj isn't allowed to touch a motherboard for two weeks <img src="images/smiles/icon_wink.gif" alt="icon_wink.gif" border="0" /></span><span class="gensmall"></span></td>
